WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:07.794
It was almost midnight when Fagin left Nancy. As the night was piercing cold, he had no temptation to loiter.

2
00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:12.103
A sharp wind scoured the streets and seemed to clear them of people.

3
00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:17.118
Fagin trembled and shivered as every fresh gust drove him on his way.

4
00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:21.443
At the corner of his own street, he fumbled in his pocket for his key.

5
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:26.118
A dark figure emerged from the shadow of a doorway and glided up to him.

6
00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:29.530
"Fagin!" whispered a voice close to his ear.

7
00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:34.935
"Ah!" The old man quickly stopped and turned around. "Is that—"

8
00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:42.356
"Yes!" interrupted the stranger. "I have been lingering here for two hours. Where have you been?"

9
00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:47.840
"On your business, my dear." Fagin glanced uneasily at his companion.

10
00:00:47.841 --> 00:00:52.086
"Oh, of course." The stranger sneered.

11
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:54.388
"Well, and what's come of it?"

12
00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:56.349
"Nothing good."

13
00:00:56.349 --> 00:01:01.332
The stranger gave Fagin a startled look. "Nothing bad, I hope?"

14
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:09.491
Fagin shook his head and started to walk again. When they arrived in front of Fagin's house, the stranger said he wanted to talk inside.

15
00:01:09.492 --> 00:01:16.054
"My blood is chilled from standing about so long," he added. "The wind was blowing right through me."

16
00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:21.975
Fagin did not want to let the man into his house, so he muttered something about not having a fire.

17
00:01:21.976 --> 00:01:29.634
But the stranger repeated his request as a demand. At last Fagin unlocked the door, and they both went inside.

18
00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:37.213
"It's as dark as a grave in here," said the man, groping forward a few steps. "Make haste with a light."

19
00:01:37.214 --> 00:01:44.626
"Shut the door," whispered Fagin from the end of the hall. As he spoke, the door closed with a loud noise.

20
00:01:44.627 --> 00:01:52.699
"That wasn't my doing," said the other man, feeling his way slowly forward. "The wind blew it closed, or it shut by itself."

21
00:01:52.700 --> 00:02:01.882
Fagin stealthily descended the stairs to the kitchen. He soon returned with a lighted candle and the news that Toby Crackit and the boys were asleep below.

22
00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:06.445
Beckoning the man to follow him, he led the way upstairs to the second floor.

23
00:02:06.446 --> 00:02:21.366
"We can say the few words we've got to say in here, my dear." Fagin threw open a door. "But there are holes in the shutters, and we don't want the neighbors to see a light. So we'll set the candle on the stairs. There!"

24
00:02:21.367 --> 00:02:26.826
With those words he placed the candle on a flight of stairs opposite the door.

25
00:02:26.827 --> 00:02:32.841
He led the way into the room, which was empty except for a broken armchair and an old couch.

26
00:02:32.842 --> 00:02:37.008
The stranger sat down on the couch with the air of a weary man.

27
00:02:37.009 --> 00:02:44.269
Fagin drew up the armchair so they sat face-to-face. The candle threw a feeble light on the opposite wall.

28
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:50.992
They spoke for some time in whispers. The stranger appeared to be in a state of considerable irritation.

29
00:02:50.993 --> 00:03:00.175
"I tell you again, the robbery was badly planned," the man said, raising his voice a little. "Why didn't you keep Oliver here and make him a pickpocket?"

30
00:03:00.176 --> 00:03:07.651
"I couldn't have done that even if I'd wanted to, my dear Monks." For it was indeed Monks whom Fagin was addressing.

31
00:03:07.652 --> 00:03:17.476
"Haven't you turned dozens of other boys into pickpockets?" Monks demanded sternly. "If you were patient for a year at most, you could get Oliver convicted.

32
00:03:17.477 --> 00:03:21.722
Then he'd be sent safely out of the country, perhaps for life." "I

33
00:03:21.723 --> 00:03:30.207
saw it was not easy to train him in the business," the old man explained. "He was not like other boys in the same circumstances." "Curse

34
00:03:30.208 --> 00:03:35.707
him, no! Or he would have been a thief long ago," muttered Monks. "I

35
00:03:35.708 --> 00:03:42.691
had no hold upon Oliver to make him worse," continued Fagin, anxiously watching the face of his companion. "I

36
00:03:42.692 --> 00:03:48.532
had nothing to frighten him with, which I always must have in the beginning or I labor in vain. What

37
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:58.437
could I do? Send him out with the Dodger and Charley? We saw how well that worked. I trembled in fear for us all when he was caught." "

38
00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:02.024
That was not my doing," observed Monks.

39
00:04:02.025 --> 00:04:04.762
"No, no, my dear.

40
00:04:04.763 --> 00:04:17.770
And I don't quarrel with it now because if it had never happened, you might never have spotted him when he was arrested. But I got him back for you by using Nancy. And then she began to grow fond of him."

41
00:04:17.771 --> 00:04:21.270
"Get rid of the girl!" Monks said impatiently.

42
00:04:21.271 --> 00:04:27.072
"That sort of thing is not our way. But don't worry," Fagin added with a smile.

43
00:04:27.073 --> 00:04:41.072
"I know how these girls are, Monks. As soon as the boy begins to harden, Nancy will care no more for him than for a block of wood. You want him turned into a thief. If he's alive, I can do it this time."

44
00:04:41.073 --> 00:04:46.477
Fagin drew nearer to Monks. "But if worst comes to worst and he's dead . . ."

45
00:04:46.478 --> 00:04:53.985
"It's no fault of mine if he is!" Monks grasped Fagin's arm with trembling hands and a look of terror.

46
00:04:53.986 --> 00:05:08.302
"Be careful, Fagin. I told you from the first that I won't shed blood. Anything but his death! If he was shot dead in that robbery, I was not the cause." Monks paused. "What's that?"

47
00:05:08.303 --> 00:05:12.953
"What?" cried Fagin as he sprung to his feet. "Where?"

48
00:05:12.954 --> 00:05:21.040
"There!" Monks pointed to the opposite wall. "A shadow! I saw the shadow of a woman in a cloak and bonnet."

49
00:05:21.041 --> 00:05:29.612
They rushed from the room. The candle, still standing where it had been placed, showed them only the empty staircase and their own white faces.

50
00:05:29.613 --> 00:05:34.534
They listened intently, but a profound silence reigned throughout the house.

51
00:05:34.535 --> 00:05:39.779
"It's your imagination," said Fagin, taking up the candle and turning to Monks.

52
00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.112
"I swear I saw a shadow," replied Monks, trembling.

53
00:05:44.113 --> 00:05:48.675
"It was bending forward when I first saw it, and when I spoke, it darted away."

54
00:05:48.676 --> 00:05:53.826
Fagin glanced contemptuously at Monks. "Follow me, if you please."

55
00:05:53.827 --> 00:06:01.580
They ascended the stairs to the third floor. They looked into all the rooms, but each one was cold, bare, and empty.

56
00:06:01.581 --> 00:06:05.255
They descended to the first floor and then to the kitchen below.

57
00:06:05.256 --> 00:06:10.247
The green mold hung upon the low walls, but all was still as death.

58
00:06:10.248 --> 00:06:20.334
"What do you think now?" asked Fagin after they had returned to the hall by the front door. "Besides ourselves, there's not a creature in the house except Toby and the boys."

59
00:06:20.335 --> 00:06:25.080
Monks shook his head and laughed grimly, saying it must have been his imagination.

60
00:06:25.081 --> 00:06:33.849
Then he declined to continue talking about Oliver because it was now past one o'clock. And so he went out into the cold night.

